# Line-ending and text/binary policy.
#
# Goal: every checkout on every OS -- Windows dev machines,
# macOS dev machines, and the Linux CI runner -- sees the
# exact same bytes for every source file. No `core.autocrlf`
# surprises, no rustfmt/prettier "your local run says clean,
# CI says not clean" flap, no line-ending noise in diffs.
#
# Policy in one sentence: **everything git decides is text is
# stored AND checked out as LF**, everywhere. The handful of
# formats that MUST be CRLF on disk (Windows batch, PowerShell,
# Visual Studio .sln) are called out explicitly; genuine
# binaries are called out to defeat the auto-detection.
#
# When adding a new file type here, prefer being explicit over
# letting `text=auto` decide -- the auto heuristic is fine but
# a bad guess on a large asset is expensive to unwind later.
#
# ## Rolling out a change
#
# After editing this file, run `git add --renormalize .` at the
# repo root and commit the result. That rewrites every tracked
# file into the canonical form so a subsequent `git diff` is
# clean on every checkout.

# --- Default: git auto-detects text vs binary, forces LF -----
* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Source code (explicit for clarity, not required) --------
*.rs           text eol=lf
*.toml         text eol=lf
*.ts           text eol=lf
*.tsx          text eol=lf
*.js           text eol=lf
*.jsx          text eol=lf
*.mjs          text eol=lf
*.cjs          text eol=lf
*.json         text eol=lf
*.yml          text eol=lf
*.yaml         text eol=lf
*.md           text eol=lf
*.css          text eol=lf
*.scss         text eol=lf
*.html         text eol=lf
*.svg          text eol=lf
*.wgsl         text eol=lf
*.glsl         text eol=lf
*.txt          text eol=lf
*.plan.md      text eol=lf

# --- Lockfiles: text but never auto-merge --------------------
# Merge conflicts in these are almost always resolved by
# regenerating, not by hand-editing. Marking `-diff` keeps
# review UIs from choking on huge lock diffs.
Cargo.lock          text eol=lf -diff
package-lock.json   text eol=lf -diff
yarn.lock           text eol=lf -diff
pnpm-lock.yaml      text eol=lf -diff

# --- Shell / config: LF is load-bearing on Unix --------------
# A `#!/bin/sh` script with CRLF fails cryptically with
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory". Pinning LF
# closes that trap for anyone who edits a script on Windows.
*.sh           text eol=lf
*.bash         text eol=lf
*.zsh          text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.editorconfig  text eol=lf
Dockerfile     text eol=lf
*.dockerfile   text eol=lf

# --- Windows-native formats: CRLF is required ----------------
# These files are consumed by tools that assume CRLF (cmd.exe,
# PowerShell in Windows contexts, Visual Studio project files).
# Forcing LF here would break them.
*.bat          text eol=crlf
*.cmd          text eol=crlf
*.ps1          text eol=crlf
*.psm1         text eol=crlf
*.sln          text eol=crlf
*.vcxproj      text eol=crlf

# --- Binaries: never touch -----------------------------------
# Explicit list so `text=auto` never misdetects and corrupts.
*.png          binary
*.jpg          binary
*.jpeg         binary
*.gif          binary
*.webp         binary
*.ico          binary
*.icns         binary
*.pdf          binary
*.zip          binary
*.gz           binary
*.tar          binary
*.7z           binary
*.mp3          binary
*.mp4          binary
*.mov          binary
*.wav          binary
*.ogg          binary
*.woff         binary
*.woff2        binary
*.ttf          binary
*.otf          binary
*.dll          binary
*.exe          binary
*.so           binary
*.dylib        binary
*.a            binary
*.lib          binary
*.bin          binary
*.wasm         binary
*.onnx         binary
*.safetensors  binary
